.TH AUDIOD 4
.SH NAME
audiod \- the audio server (port "audio")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR audio .
.B /bin/audiod
is started on demand by
.BR init (8),
with device rights, by its first user, then kept (the volume).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B audiod
drives Intel High Definition Audio controllers (QEMU's intel-hda and
ich9-intel-hda, PC chipsets, an NVIDIA GPU's HDMI one) and their codecs.
It has one output and one input stream: 48 kHz, 16-bit, stereo, 4 bytes a
frame.
The output goes to the speakers, to an HDMI or DisplayPort monitor with
sound, or to Bluetooth headphones (each write then goes on to
.BR btd (4)).
.PP
Each stream is a 128 KiB ring in DMA memory: a write lands by IPC straight
in the ring the controller plays from; a read is answered straight out of
the one it records into.
What has been played is zeroed, so it plays silence when writes stop
coming, and stops a moment after all is played.
Recording runs while someone reads.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B AUDIO_WRITE
sbuf = frames; playing starts. \-> bytes taken (0: there was no room; the
reply waited for some: send again).
.TP
.B AUDIO_READ
w[1] = rbuf's size; recording starts \-> bytes in rbuf (waits for some).
.TP
.B AUDIO_VOL
w[1] = the volume 0-100, | AUDIO_MUTE; AUDIO_ASK: no change \-> the volume.
.TP
.B AUDIO_OUT
w[1] = the output (0 the speakers, 1... monitors with sound, then Bluetooth
headphones), AUDIO_BT, or AUDIO_ASK \-> the one playing, reply w[1] = how
many; rbuf: their names, one a line.
.TP
.B AUDIO_KEY
w[1] = a media key (MK_PLAY, MK_PAUSE, MK_STOP, MK_NEXT, MK_PREV, MK_VOLUP,
MK_VOLDOWN; from btd: a headset's buttons). Pause holds the writer's
AUDIO_WRITE until play; stop, next, previous: its next reply has w[1] = the
key. \-> 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR play (1),
.BR btd (4)
.SH BUGS
No mixing of several programs, no other rates (convert files beforehand).
No jack interrupts, no microphone choice (the first one wired), no record
level.
